<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Upper Egypt</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> is the southern part of Egypt, the narrow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-valley">Nile Valley</a> stretching from around Memphis southwards to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-cataract">First Cataract</a> at Aswan. It was home to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, the religious centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>. By around 1100 BCE the High Priests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> governed Upper Egypt as virtually independent rulers, and the region later came under the control of the Kushite kings of the Twenty-fifth Dynasty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/upper-egypt.jpg" alt="Upper Egypt" width="216" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeff Dahl · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Upper_Egypt_Nomes.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…by establishing Islam as the religion of the state, encouraging the immigration of literate scholars and merchants from Bornu and Bagirmi, and developing the Darb al-Arba'in or Darb el-Arba'in (the “Forty Days Road”), the desert trade route linking Darfur's commercial centre at Kobbei to Asyut in <b>Upper Egypt</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/darfur-sultanate">Darfur Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>By approximately 1080 BCE, the High Priest of Amun Herihor governed <b>Upper Egypt</b> as a de facto independent ruler, while Smendes controlled Lower Egypt from Tanis.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the History of the Patriarchs of Alexandria, King Kyriakos marched into <b>Upper Egypt</b> around 747 to demand the release of the imprisoned Coptic patriarch, and Arab historians report that Nubian forces raided Upper Egypt as far as Akhmim in the 960s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/makuria">Makuria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>While this broadened the military base, it also shifted the balance of power within society, and the great native revolt in <b>Upper Egypt</b> (206–186 BCE) followed soon afterwards.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ptolemaic-empire">Ptolemaic Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…641 and 642, Arabic gradually replaced it: Arabic became the language of administration in 706, Coptic Christians increasingly spoke and wrote Arabic from the 10th century, and Coptic seems to have died out as a spoken language, last in villages of <b>Upper Egypt</b>, by about the 17th century.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-egyptian">Ancient Egyptian · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Coptic was the majority language of Egypt until some time between the 10th and 12th centuries, then lived on as a minority first language until at least the 17th century, and perhaps in isolated pockets of <b>Upper Egypt</b> as late as the 19th.</blockquote><a href="/language/coptic">Coptic · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li></ul></section>
